  "editorReview": {
    "text": "In the fluorescent hush of the 1962 Manchester telephone exchange, this procedural achieves a rare tactile immersion. Ruth Calloway is a triumph of moral complexity; her journey from rigid ledger-keeper to guardian of complicit silence is deeply felt, while the fair-play clueing—specifically the unclosed 'C' loop and the cancelled bus timetable—rewards the sharpest readers. However, the mid-section suffers from rhythmic fatigue, where repetitive sentence structures and an over-reliance on em-dashes stall the tension. Though the emotional resolution lands with weight, the deflation of Alec Dunb",
    "penName": "疏影",
    "title": "Fiction Editor",
    "rating": "Worth reading"
